  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 303 AM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nJust to recap yesterday, MSP recorded an 81 degree dewpoint at\n2AM (1 degree shy of the all time record) and then after \nmixing/drying out during the afternoon, it recorded our first \n100 degree day since June of 2022. In a sadistic coincidence, \nyesterday was sandwiched between two days where the daily record\nis 100F, but had a 104F daily record itself. So we came up \nshort of a daily record, but it was still a fun day for weather \nnerds in the Twin Cities. Eau Claire peaked at 95F, 7 degrees \nshort of its daily record (102), and St Cloud had a high of 97F,\n5 degrees short of its daily record (102).\n\nWe remain hot today, with perhaps a chance to over-perform \nagain in some spots as dew points sit in the 60s. Winds will be\nfairly light as a stationary boundary sits across the Dakotas \nand southwest MN, and high pressure sprawls out just to the \neast. There continues to be at least some indication of \npotential for scattered showers to bubble along the boundary in \nsouthwest MN this afternoon. The boundary and afternoon storm \nchances then shift to east-central MN and northwest WI for \nWednesday. Temperatures should also be quite warm again for \nWednesday, with heat indices in the low to mid 90s.\n\nThursday into Friday remains our best chance of organized\nrainfall, though there are some reasons to be cautious. \nDespite PWATs being quite notable (1.6\"+), the main surface low \nmay develop further south into southern IA or northern MO. That \nputs the Upper MS Valley in a region of weaker forcing and less \nwidespread heavy rainfall. To play devil's advocate to myself \nthough, the latest deterministic runs of the both the Euro and \nGFS show a northern solution with QPF amount of 1\"+ across \nsouthern MN. Given our recent inability to see any appreciable \nrainfall, I am admittedly more pessimistic than usual. At the \nvery least, this highlights an area of uncertainty with this \nshortwave. It is worth noting too, the SPC has introduced a \nMarginal risk for severe weather across western MN for Day 3. \nAny potential for storms and severe weather will likely depend \non outflow boundary interaction and cold pool development.\n\nBehind this system, a nice few days of closer-to-normal\ntemperatures are expected. We do warm up again early next \nweek, though a building shortwave may attempt to flatten out the\nseemingly never ending ridge across the western CONUS. \nDepending on how this pattern evolves, it may result in our next\nchance for organized precipitation.",
